  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA strong and large area of low pressure centered near the\nDelmarva early this afternoon will push north-northwest to WNY\nby Friday morning as it occludes. Strong forcing along an\noccluding frontal boundary northeast of the sfc low will \ncontinue to push rain north across NEPA & CNY through the \nafternoon and into the evening. Combined with a strong LLJ of \n80+ kts and a surge of moisture off the Atlantic, steady \nrainfall will become moderate to heavy at times across NEPA &\nCNY. Rainfall amounts of 0.50-1.50 inches is expected, with the\nlower range expected for area from I-81 to the Catskills where\nsome downsloping and the expectation of a dry slot will cut down\non rainfall amounts. Rainfall over area west of I-81 to the\nFinger Lakes will be toward the higher end of that range. Any\nrainfall at this point is beneficial, especially where higher\namounts are expected. These areas are currently in a D1 \nModerate Drought. Steady rain is expected through the evening,\nbefore steady rain transitions to showers. Showers will linger\nthrough most of Friday with less than a tenth of an inch of\naccumulation expected. \n\nWith the strong LLJ of 80+ kts crossing the region, strong sfc \nwinds are expected through this evening. Winds to 60 MPH are \npossible over portions of NEPA where High Wind Warnings are in \nplace for Wyoming, Lackawanna and Luzerne counties. Further \nnorth for the rest of NEPA & for CNY, Wind Advisories are in \nplace for all but the Western Finger Lakes counties and Northern\nOneida County. Areas in the Wind Advisories can expect wind \ngusts in the 45 to 50 MPH range. While strong winds are \nexpected, much stronger winds are not expected to mix down to \nthe surface. This is thanks to an inversion that will be in \nplace around 900 hPa and higher. Localized stronger winds will \nbe possible across the higher terrain areas, closer to the \nheight of the LLJ. Wind headlines are in effect until 1AM \ntonight with winds expected to weaken prior to this time as the \nstrong LLJ moves northeast of the area.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2....\n\nAnother weather system is expected Sunday night into Monday. \nThe low looks to track just west or right over the area early \nMonday morning with a period of rain likely. Temperatures will \nbe mild, so it is expected to be all rain Sunday night. Some \ncooler air will gradually filter into the area on Monday behind \nthe cold front, so some of the lingering rain showers could \nperhaps mix with some wet snow flakes later Monday into Monday \nnight before ending. Temperatures will still be mild in the \nmid-40s to around 50 on Monday though. It cools off into the \nupper 20s to lower 30s by Monday night.\n\nHigh pressure builds in from the west on Tuesday into Wednesday next \nweek, so other than some lingering lake effect rain and/or snow\nshowers on Tuesday it should dry out under partly sunny skies \nwith seasonable temperatures.",
